        {
         "id": "v2-14b8f-p29-practice-j8-042605-j8-a",
         "type": "part",
         "label": "a",
         "prompt": "Show that $\\displaystyle\\int_{-2}^{2}x^4\\,dx=2\\int_0^2x^4\\,dx$.",
         "answerSpaceMm": 40,
         "answer": {
          "short": "Both sides equal $\\dfrac{64}{5}$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\int_{-2}^{2}x^4\\,dx&=\\left[\\frac{x^5}{5}\\right]_{-2}^{2}=\\frac{32}{5}+\\frac{32}{5}=\\frac{64}{5}\\\\2\\int_0^2x^4\\,dx&=2\\left[\\frac{x^5}{5}\\right]_0^2=2\\times\\frac{32}{5}=\\frac{64}{5}\\end{align*}$$"
         }
        },
        {
         "id": "v2-14b8f-p29-practice-j8-042605-j8-b",
         "type": "part",
         "label": "b",
         "prompt": "Show that $\\displaystyle\\int_{-2}^{2}x^3\\,dx=0$.",
         "answerSpaceMm": 30,
         "answer": {
          "short": "$\\left[\\dfrac{x^4}{4}\\right]_{-2}^{2}=4-4=0$",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\int_{-2}^{2}x^3\\,dx&=\\left[\\frac{x^4}{4}\\right]_{-2}^{2}\\\\&=4-4\\\\&=0\\end{align*}$$"
         }
        },
        {
         "id": "v2-14b8f-p29-practice-j8-042605-j8-c",
         "type": "part",
         "label": "c",
         "prompt": {
          "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
          "version": 1,
          "blocks": [
           {
            "id": "v2-14b8f-p29-practice-j8-042605-j8-c-d1",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
             {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Explain, using the symmetry of the graphs of ",
              "marks": []
             },
             {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "y=x^4",
              "display": false
             },
             {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " and ",
              "marks": []
             },
             {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "y=x^3",
              "display": false
             },
             {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ", why the results in parts ",
              "marks": []
             },
             {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "a",
              "marks": [
               "bold"
              ]
             },
             {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " and ",
              "marks": []
             },
             {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "b",
              "marks": [
               "bold"
              ]
             },
             {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " hold.",
              "marks": []
             }
            ]
           }
          ]
         },
         "answerSpaceMm": 35,
         "answer": {
          "short": "$y=x^4$ is symmetric about the $y$-axis, so the area on $[-2,0]$ equals the area on $[0,2]$. $y=x^3$ has half-turn symmetry about the origin, so the signed area on $[-2,0]$ is the negative of that on $[0,2]$.",
          "worked": "The graph of $y=x^4$ is a mirror image in the $y$-axis, so the region from $-2$ to $0$ matches the region from $0$ to $2$. Hence the integral over $[-2,2]$ is twice the integral over $[0,2]$.\n\nThe graph of $y=x^3$ looks the same after a half-turn about the origin. The region from $-2$ to $0$ lies below the $x$-axis and has the same area as the region from $0$ to $2$ above it, so the signed areas cancel and the integral is $0$."
         }
        },
        {
         "id": "v2-14b8f-p29-practice-j8-042605-j8-d",
         "type": "part",
         "label": "d",
         "prompt": "Use symmetry to evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_{-2}^{2}(x^5+4x^3+3x^2)\\,dx$.",
         "answerSpaceMm": 45,
         "answer": {
          "short": "$16$",
          "worked": "The terms $x^5$ and $4x^3$ have half-turn symmetry about the origin, so they integrate to $0$ over $[-2,2]$. The term $3x^2$ is symmetric about the $y$-axis.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\int_{-2}^{2}(x^5+4x^3+3x^2)\\,dx&=0+2\\int_0^2 3x^2\\,dx\\\\&=2\\left[x^3\\right]_0^2\\\\&=16\\end{align*}$$"
         }
        }
       ]
