    {
     "op": "theory",
     "blockId": "p25-definition-notes",
     "replacement": "Write $F(b)-F(a)$ as $\\left[F(x)\\right]_a^b$.\n\nAlways show the substitution. Put brackets whenever you subtract two or more terms.\n\nYou can factor a constant out of a definite integral and out of square brackets. Multiplying all $y$ values by $k$ multiplies the definite integral by $k$.\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\int_a^b kf(x)\\,dx&=k\\int_a^b f(x)\\,dx\\\\\\left[kF(x)\\right]_a^b&=k\\left[F(x)\\right]_a^b\\end{aligned}$$\n\nYou do not need to add $+C$ when evaluating a definite integral, since it cancels in the subtraction.",
     "autoApply": true,
     "key": "theory|p25-definition-notes",
     "targetFingerprint": "4fc4a15ebf4c829e88bba2c25d4bf396e99f527ec064a0f8ebb27e7173feff81",
     "applied": true
    }
